# Comprehensive Learning Guide: Interacting with APIs in JavaScript

## Course Overview

This course teaches you how to fetch, parse, and manipulate data from external APIs using JavaScript. You'll learn to build real-world applications that communicate with web services, handle asynchronous operations, and manage responses gracefully.

**Who it's for:**
- JavaScript developers transitioning from static scripts to dynamic, data-driven applications
- Full-stack and frontend developers preparing for technical interviews
- Anyone building web applications that rely on third-party services

**Why it matters for interview prep:**
API interaction is fundamental to modern web development. Interviewers expect you to:
- Write clean, error-handling code for HTTP requests
- Understand async/await and Promise patterns
- Debug network issues and handle edge cases
- Build features that depend on external data sources
Mastery here demonstrates practical, production-ready thinking.

---

## Core Topics Covered

- **Fetch API & HTTP Methods** — GET, POST, PUT, DELETE requests; understanding status codes and headers
- **Promises & Async/Await** — Writing non-blocking, readable asynchronous code
- **Error Handling & Validation** — Try/catch blocks, network error recovery, data validation patterns
- **Data Transformation & Parsing** — JSON parsing, extracting relevant fields, normalizing responses
- **Request Configuration** — Headers, authentication tokens, CORS, query parameters
- **Rate Limiting & Throttling** — Managing API quota, preventing request overload
- **Caching Strategies** — Local storage, in-memory caching, cache invalidation
- **Testing & Mocking APIs** — Unit testing with mock data, testing network failures

---

## Learning Path Workflow

### **Phase 1: Fundamentals (Tasks 1–15)**
1. Understand the HTTP request/response cycle
2. Learn Fetch API basics (simple GET requests)
3. Parse JSON responses
4. Handle basic errors with try/catch
5. Introduction to Promises and `.then()` chains

**Checkpoint:** You can fetch data from a public API and log it to the console.

---

### **Phase 2: Async/Await & Control Flow (Tasks 16–30)**
6. Convert Promise chains to async/await syntax
7. Handle multiple sequential requests
8. Manage parallel requests with `Promise.all()`
9. Implement timeout mechanisms
10. Chain dependent API calls

**Checkpoint:** You can orchestrate complex, multi-step API interactions cleanly.

---

### **Phase 3: Request Customization & Headers (Tasks 31–45)**
11. Add custom headers (Authorization, Content-Type)
12. Send query parameters and URL encoding
13. Implement POST, PUT, DELETE requests
14. Handle request bodies (JSON payloads)
15. Work with authentication (API keys, Bearer tokens)

**Checkpoint:** You can interact with protected APIs and mutate remote data.

---

### **Phase 4: Advanced Patterns (Tasks 46–60)**
16. Implement retry logic for failed requests
17. Build request queuing and throttling
18. Cache responses intelligently
19. Handle pagination
20. Implement search, filtering, and sorting from API data

**Checkpoint:** You can build production-like resilience into API layers.

---

### **Phase 5: Testing & Real-World Integration (Tasks 61–70)**
21. Mock APIs with libraries (jest.mock, sinon)
22. Test error paths and edge cases
23. Build a small project combining all patterns
24. Optimize performance and minimize requests
25. Debug network issues with browser DevTools

**Checkpoint:** You can write tested, performant API integration code.

---

## Key Skills You'll Build

| Skill | What You Can Do |
|-------|-----------------|
| **Fetch API mastery** | Write efficient HTTP requests with proper configuration |
| **Async/await fluency** | Write readable, non-blocking code without callback hell |
| **Error handling** | Gracefully handle network failures, timeouts, and bad data |
| **Data transformation** | Shape API responses to fit your application needs |
| **State management** | Manage loading, error, and success states in your app |
| **Security awareness** | Handle sensitive data (tokens, API keys) responsibly |
| **Testing & debugging** | Write testable code and diagnose network issues |
| **Performance optimization** | Minimize requests, cache intelligently, parallelize work |

---

## Sample Problem Types

### **Problem Type 1: Simple Data Fetching**

**Description:** Fetch data from a public API, parse the response, and display it.

```javascript
// Task example: Fetch a GitHub user's profile
async function fetchGitHubUser(username) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.github.com/users/${username}`);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
    const user = await response.json();
    console.log(`${user.name} has ${user.public_repos} repos`);
    return user;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to fetch user:', error.message);
  }
}

fetchGitHubUser('torvalds');
```

**Key concepts:** Fetch, JSON parsing, error handling, checking response status.

---

### **Problem Type 2: Multiple Requests & Data Merging**

**Description:** Combine data from multiple API endpoints, then transform and return merged result.

```javascript
// Task example: Get a user and their recent repos, merge into single object
async function getUserWithRepos(username) {
  try {
    const userRes = await fetch(`https://api.github.com/users/${username}`);
    const reposRes = await fetch(`https://api.github.com/users/${username}/repos`);

    if (!userRes.ok || !reposRes.ok) throw new Error('API error');

    const user = await userRes.json();
    const repos = await reposRes.json();

    return {
      name: user.name,
      bio: user.bio,
      topRepos: repos.slice(0, 5).map(r => r.name)
    };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error:', error.message);
    return null;
  }
}
```

**Key concepts:** Multiple requests, Promise.all() (optimization), data transformation, error handling.

---

### **Problem Type 3: POST Requests with Authentication**

**Description:** Send data to an API with headers, handle response validation.

```javascript
// Task example: Create a new post with authentication
async function createPost(title, body, authToken) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        'Authorization': `Bearer ${authToken}`
      },
      body: JSON.stringify({ title, body, userId: 1 })
    });

    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
    const newPost = await response.json();
    return newPost;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to create post:', error.message);
    throw error;
  }
}
```

**Key concepts:** Method, headers, request body, authorization, status validation.

---

### **Problem Type 4: Retry Logic & Rate Limiting**

**Description:** Implement resilience: retry failed requests, respect rate limits.

```javascript
// Task example: Fetch with exponential backoff retry
async function fetchWithRetry(url, maxRetries = 3) {
  let lastError;

  for (let i = 0; i < maxRetries; i++) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
      return await response.json();
    } catch (error) {
      lastError = error;
      if (i < maxRetries - 1) {
        const delay = Math.pow(2, i) * 1000; // 1s, 2s, 4s
        await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay));
      }
    }
  }

  throw lastError;
}

fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data');
```

**Key concepts:** Retry loops, exponential backoff, error recovery, resilience.

---

